WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.220
So, the American Library Association's Office for Intellectual Freedom just released it's list

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.880
of the ten most challenged books of the year in the United States.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.280
'Challenged' meaning someone requested to have the book removed from the school or library

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.360
And it turns out that the most challenged book of 2015 was "Looking For Alaska"

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.380
which was written by me

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:24.200
The book has been challenged and banned around the country for 'offensive language' and 'sexually explicit' descriptions

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.760
I suppose this is a kind of honor, I mean Looking For Alaska contained the very same offensive language

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:33.260
and sexually explicit descriptions as it did ten years ago, but was much less likely to be banned

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.960
because, you know, not many people had read it.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.280
Also, Looking For Alaska beat some of my favorite books, like 'Two Boys Kissing' by David Levithan

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.620
and 'Fun House' by Alison Bechdel

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.660
and also the Holy Bible, which came in 6th.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:46.900
To be fair, the Bible does contain it's fair share of explicit passages

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.580
Anyway, I'm often asked to respond to the banning of Looking For Alaska from schools and libraries, so okay.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:52.900
Here is my response:

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:57.200
Text is meaningless without context, and what usually happens with Looking For Alaska is

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.860
that a parent chose one particular page of the novel to an administrator and then the book gets banned

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:07.160
without anyone, who objects to it, having read more then that one particular page.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:11.580
The scene in question involves a very awkward and ultimately failed attempt at oral sex

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:18.320
which is described in very cold and clinical language, in fact the entire passage contains only one adjective: nervous

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.980
And then in the book's next scene, two characters have a much more sensually described and passionate

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.200
but much less sexually explicit interaction.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.680
That passage ends: "We didn't have sex, we never got naked, I never touched her bare breasts,

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:36.940
and her hands never got lower than my hips. It didn't matter. As she slept, I whispered: "I love you, Alaska Young"

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:40.220
So in context, the novel is arguing, really in a rather pointed way,

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:45.900
that emotionally intimate kissing can be a whole lot more fulfilling than emotionally empty oral sex.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.580
Teenagers are critically engaged and thoughtful readers

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:53.440
They do not read Looking For Alaska and think: "I should go have some agressively unerotic oral sex."

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.000
And they also don't read 'The Outsiders' and think: "I should join a gang."

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.160
Or read 'Divergent' and think: "I should jump onto moving trains."

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.960
So as far as I can tell, that kind of narrow perscriptive reading seems only to happen inside

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:04.780
the offices of school superintendents.

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:09.240
So yeah, I don't think Looking For Alaska is pornographic, and I don't think it's readers find it titillating.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:13.840
But that noted, I don't think it should be up to me whether Looking For Alaska, or actually any book

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.320
is in a school or a library. Because I am not a teacher or a librarian.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.140
The highly trained, criminally underpaid professionals we'd employed to make those decisions.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:28.380
As I've discussed in the past, I don't think publicly  funded learning exists primarily for the benefit of parents

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.580
or even primarily for the benefit of students.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:32.780
It exists for the benefit of the social order.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:36.220
We are all better of with a well-informed, well-educated population

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:40.500
because that population is more likely to start successful businesses and develop treatments for cancer.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.120
And write musicals about treasury secretaries.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.360
And that's why everyone pays taxes, to fund public schools and libraries,

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:49.980
regardless of whether you personally use the library or have kids in school

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:54.080
I think teachers and librarians know more about teaching and librarianship than I do.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.680
And I believe they must be allowed to do their jobs, serving the whole public.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.980
But even beyond that, I don't believe that books, even bad books, corrupt us.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.560
Instead I believe books challenge and interrogate, they give us windows into the lives of others

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.200
and give us mirrors so that we can better see ourselves.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.760
And ultimately, if you have a worldview that can be undone by a novel

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:16.540
Let me submit that the problem is not with the novel...

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:18.080
Hank, I'll see you on Friday

